WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.260
Swiss subsidies paid farmers per grazed hectare to keep alpine meadows alive, yet the money

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:11.640
quietly emptied the steep slopes instead. This is unintended consequences, episode 118.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:18.340
In the summer of 2015, a farmer in the canton of Growbundin moved his entire herd down

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:24.440
to the flat valley floor near Thusis, leaving the steeper pastures above to grow rank. The

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:29.240
payments he received were calculated strictly by the hectares his animals actually grazed,

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.960
So the choice made simple economic sense.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.740
This was designed to maintain open meadows and the species that depend on them.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.880
Instead, it accelerated the very abandonment it sought to prevent.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:47.800
Swiss agricultural policy after the 1990s reforms sought to replace older price support systems

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.760
with direct payments tied to observable land use.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:56.680
Lawmakers and agricultural economists wanted to keep the distinctive patchwork of alpine meadows

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.620
that had developed over centuries of summer pasturing.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.280
They wanted this both for biodiversity and for the scenic character that supports tourism.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.180
At the time, the approach looked straightforward.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.620
Link public money to the continued presence of livestock on the land, and the meadows would

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:14.300
remain open.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.360
The policy drew on long-standing Swiss traditions of communal alpine management.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:24.400
It also drew on emerging European ideas that environmental goals could be met through

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.560
area-based incentives rather than prescriptive rules.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.600
Officials viewed the measure as a practical compromise between supporting farm incomes

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:35.020
and delivering measurable public goods.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.160
No one expected the payments to favor only the most accessible ground.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:44.400
The reasoning rested on the assumption that a uniform rate per hectare would function like

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.760
a broad signal across all terrain types.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:52.600
Earlier commodity price supports had encouraged intensification without regard to landscape

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.080
pattern.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:58.080
The new direct payments appeared to correct that distortion by making the simple act of

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.800
keeping animals on grass financially visible.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:06.600
Proponents calculated that if total grazed area stayed constant or grew, the ecological

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.560
outcome would follow automatically.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:13.980
They did not model the labor differential between moving a herd across gentle valley fields

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.660
versus driving the same animals up narrow rocky tracks each morning and evening.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:25.320
That omission left the policy exposed once individual farms began optimizing within the payment formula.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:33.040
The direct payment system was phased in through the agricultural act revisions of the late 1990s and early 2000s.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:37.560
Per hectare grazing premiums became a central component of federal support.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.860
Early evaluations noted that total livestock numbers held steady.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:46.020
Some valley farms continued to claim payments for higher pastures.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:51.460
Proponents pointed to the visible continuation of summer grazing on many accessible sites.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.200
They argued that the link between payment and land use would reward stewardship.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:03.100
A few agronomists warned that uniform per-hector rates ignored differences in terrain cost, yet

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:07.820
the objection received little traction amid broad political support for the new environmental

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.060
contract with farmers.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:15.380
By the mid-2000s, the program had become a stable, multi-hundred million frank line in the

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:21.820
federal budget. Implementation proceeded Canton by Canton. Federal guidelines set the base rate,

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:27.500
and Canton's added modest top-ups for particular practices. Because the core payment scaled

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:33.320
directly with declared hectares, administrators could verify claims through aerial imagery and farm

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:39.160
registers without needing to inspect slope angles or walking times. That administrative simplicity

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:45.060
help secure parliamentary approval. Early monitoring reports focused on aggregate-grazed area,

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:50.720
rather than on the distribution of grazing days across elevation bans. So the first signs of

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:56.220
concentration remained invisible in the official statistics. Because steeper slopes require more

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:02.060
labor and yield less forage per hour of work, many farm families found it rational to concentrate

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:07.680
animals on the flatter, more productive valley parcels, while still claiming the full per hectare

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:13.780
payment. Over successive seasons, the steeper parcels received fewer grazing days than none at all.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.720
Without regular defoliation, shrubs and young trees quickly colonized the ground.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:22.740
They converted former meadow into dense forest within a decade.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.700
Bio-diversity surveys later documented losses of light-dependent meadow plants and insects on

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:34.360
precisely those slopes the policy had aimed to protect. The spatial concentration also reduced

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:39.240
the mosaic of short and tall vegetation that many specialist species require.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:45.500
This amplified the habitat loss beyond the simple area abandoned. Because payments scaled with

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:51.580
claimed hectares rather than with ecological outcome, total subsidy outlays continued to rise even

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:57.180
as the heterogeneity the payments were meant to preserve declined. Second order effects appeared in

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:02.220
the form of heavier machinery traffic on the remaining valley pastures and reduced traditional

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:08.120
maintenance of remote alpine huts. Third order effects included shifts in local tourism patterns

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:13.660
as hikers encountered fewer open vistas and more closed woodland on mid elevation trails.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:18.960
The arithmetic was straightforward once examined at the farm level. A farmer could

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:24.780
graze 20 hectares of valley floor with the same daily labor previously spread across 30 hectares

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:30.260
of mixed terrain. The payment for the 20 hectares remained attractive because it required fewer

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:33.080
person hours and lower fuel costs for transport.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:38.380
As more families made the same calculation, the abandoned parcels lost the repeated

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:41.760
trampling and browsing that had kept woody regrowth in check.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:47.060
Within five to seven years, the first pioneer shrubs reached heights that excluded the light

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:51.740
dependent forbs and butterflies, the original policy language had listed as targets.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:57.280
Because the payment formula contained no multiplier for slope or for minimum stocking density

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:02.160
on difficult ground, the incentive to maintain the full original pattern simply disappeared

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:03.380
from the farm budget sheet.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:09.760
By the early 2010s, federal and cantinal agencies began commissioning targeted studies on past

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.300
your abandonment and its biodiversity costs.

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:17.940
Some cantons introduced supplementary payments for difficult terrain and for minimum stocking

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:23.500
densities on steep ground, yet uptake remained uneven because the new top-ups still had to

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:25.660
compete with the simpler area-based system.

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:31.480
In a few regions, farmers formed cooperatives to share labor on remote pastures, but these

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:34.320
arrangements covered only a fraction of the abandoned land.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:39.960
The core per hectare grazing payment structure was retained, now accompanied by monitoring

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.900
requirements and occasional ecological set-asides.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:48.280
Today, the pattern of valley concentration persists, though at a slower rate than in the first

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:49.260
decade of the payments.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.200
Incentive structures that reward a single observable action will reliably direct effort toward the

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:00.320
lowest cost way of producing that action, even when the original goal is landscape-wide.

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:06.440
Complex ecological outcomes, such as habitat heterogeneity, cannot be secured by uniform

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:07.720
area payments alone.

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:13.220
They require either differentiated rates or direct conditionality on the features that matter.

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:18.400
When designing support for land stewardship, it is useful to ask whether the metric being

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:23.280
paid will still produce the desired pattern once every rational actor optimizes against

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:23.560
it.

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:28.800
How might similar area-based incentives in other countries be quietly reshaping their

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.300
own rural landscapes today?

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:33.440
That's unintended consequences for today.

